@startuml
title Protocol Emulator ASIC — planned architecture
top to bottom direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

package "Host / board" {
  component "Host computer" as host
  component "Protocol wires" as wires
}

package "Tiny Tapeout top" {
  component "Passive SPI loader\npe_ctrl" as loader
  package "Programmable SoC — pe_soc" {
    component "CPU\nfirmware engine" as cpu
    component "Instruction SRAM\n1024 × 16" as imem
    component "CPU data memory\n16-byte register array" as dmem
    component "Frame buffer SRAM\n2 KB" as fbuf
    component "Tick timer\n60 MHz timebase" as tick
    component "Pin matrix\nper-pin OUT / OE / IN / OD" as pinmux

    package "Integrated baseline: 10BASE-T receive" {
      component "DRU\noversampled capture" as dru
      component "Manchester decoder" as manch_rx
      component "Ethernet RX MAC" as eth_rx
      component "CRC-32 check" as crc_rx
    }

    package "Planned shared word engine" {
      component "Indexed control window\nIO port 0xF; 8-bit data" as window
      component "Programmable timing\nencoded-cell + half-cell + payload strobes" as timing
      component "pe_serdes\n32-bit word engine\ntx_bit_en / rx_bit_en (split)" as serdes
      component "pe_codec_mux TX\nNRZI / Manchester / stuffing" as codec_tx
      component "pe_codec_mux RX\nNRZI / Manchester / stuffing" as codec_rx
      component "Per-pin level overlay\nbefore OE / open-drain gate" as overlay
    }

    package "Separate planned Ethernet TX consumer" {
      component "Frame source\nfirmware stream or TX buffer" as tx_source
      component "Frame + preamble/SFD" as tx_frame
      component "FCS generation" as tx_crc
      component "IFG / transmit control" as tx_control
    }
  }
  component "Pads\nui_in / uo_out / uio" as pads
}

host --> loader : SPI program load
loader --> imem : host write port
imem --> cpu : instruction fetch
cpu <--> dmem : load / store
cpu <--> tick : timed firmware I/O
cpu <--> pinmux : pin-port reads / writes
pinmux <--> pads : levels, enables, inputs
pads <--> wires : board connections

wires --> dru : 10BASE-T receive
dru --> manch_rx : recovered half-bits
manch_rx --> eth_rx : decoded bits
eth_rx --> crc_rx : received frame
eth_rx --> fbuf : store-and-forward
fbuf --> cpu : firmware frame window

cpu <--> window : indexed control / data
cpu --> timing : divisor and mode
timing --> codec_tx : encoded-cell strobe + half_phase
timing --> codec_rx : decoded-cell strobe (plain modes)
timing --> serdes : tx_bit_en / rx_bit_en (payload-only)
serdes --> codec_tx : TX bits
codec_tx --> serdes : tx_stuffed (hold)
codec_tx --> overlay : encoded TX level
overlay --> pinmux : override level before OE gate
dru --> codec_rx : dru.bit_en (per decoded cell) + halves
codec_rx --> serdes : rx_bit / rx_bit_valid (skip stuff cells)

tx_source --> tx_frame
tx_frame --> tx_crc
tx_crc --> tx_control
tx_control --> serdes : payload + FCS

note bottom of pads
  Baseline firmware personas: UART, SPI master, I2C master.
  The passive loader is a separate SPI path used to load instruction SRAM.
  Current aliases: uo_out[0] is UART TX / SPI SCLK; ui_in[0] is UART RX /
  SPI MISO; uio[0:1] are I2C SDA/SCL; uio[2:3] are SPI MOSI/CS_N.
  ui_in[2] is 10BASE-T RX; ui_in[3:5] are loader SCLK/MOSI/CS_N.
end note

note bottom of serdes
  One pe_serdes instance, but its single bit_en clocks both sides; the plan
  splits it into tx_bit_en/rx_bit_en. TX advances only on payload cells
  (serdes.tx_bit_en = tx_codec_cell_en && !tx_stuffed); RX captures only
  payload cells (serdes.rx_bit_en = rx_codec_cell_en && rx_bit_valid). The
  codec bit_en is per encoded cell, never per half-cell; half_phase is an
  independent 2x half-cell level. First milestone is a wire loopback (plain
  and stuffed Manchester) through TX codec, pads/model, DRU/codec RX, and
  SERDES RX; it needs no Ethernet frame layer. The full 10BASE-T TX consumer
  is a separate plan and needs a frame source, preamble/SFD, FCS, and
  inter-frame-gap control.
  Low-speed USB and CAN are later personas; JTAG, SWD, and PS/2 are optional.
end note

@enduml
